Lab 1: Control Plane Overview and Service Introduction
Objectives
Understand the architecture and components of OpenStack's control plane
Identify key services and their responsibilities in OpenStack
Examine service interdependencies between Keystone, Nova, and Neutron
Analyze service communication patterns and management
Prerequisites
Basic Linux command line proficiency
OpenStack deployment (DevStack or all-in-one installation recommended)
SSH access to OpenStack controller node
Python 3.x installed
OpenStack CLI tools (openstack, nova, neutron commands)
Lab Environment Setup
DevStack Installation (if not already available):
git clone https://opendev.org/openstack/devstack
cd devstack
./stack.sh
Verify Installation:
source ~/devstack/openrc admin admin
openstack service list
Lab Tasks
Task 1: Identify Key Control Plane Services
Subtask 1.1: List OpenStack Services
openstack service list --long
Expected Output:

+----------------------------------+-----------+------------------+---------+---------+-----------+
| ID                               | Name      | Type             | Enabled | State   | Description |
+----------------------------------+-----------+------------------+---------+---------+-----------+
| 03d80ab240a8456d8dee6674b7d7e7d1 | nova      | compute          | True    | up      | Nova Compute Service |
| 1753438f3908430d8de6e6d920fa6f1a | neutron   | network          | True    | up      | Neutron Networking Service |
| 2d7a9a1a3b1b4b5d9e6f7c8d9e0f1a2b | keystone  | identity         | True    | up      | OpenStack Identity |
| ...                              | ...       | ...              | ...     | ...     | ...       |
+----------------------------------+-----------+------------------+---------+---------+-----------+
Key Observations:

Keystone provides identity services
Nova manages compute resources
Neutron handles networking
Subtask 1.2: Verify Service Endpoints
openstack endpoint list
Troubleshooting Tip: If endpoints are missing, recreate them using:

openstack endpoint create --region RegionOne <service> public http://<controller>:<port>
Task 2: Understand Service Interdependencies
Subtask 2.1: Keystone Authentication Flow
Get authentication token:
openstack token issue
Examine token contents:
import jwt
token = "your_token_here"
decoded = jwt.decode(token, options={"verify_signature": False})
print(decoded)
Key Concept: Services validate tokens with Keystone before processing requests.

Subtask 2.2: Nova-Keystone Integration
Check Nova service catalog:
nova-manage api_db list
Verify Nova is using Keystone:
grep auth_strategy /etc/nova/nova.conf
Expected Output: auth_strategy = keystone

Subtask 2.3: Neutron-Keystone Integration
Check Neutron configuration:
grep auth /etc/neutron/neutron.conf
Test Neutron API access:
neutron agent-list
Task 3: Service Communication Analysis
Subtask 3.1: Examine Message Queue (RabbitMQ)
sudo rabbitmqctl list_queues
sudo rabbitmqctl list_exchanges
Key Concept: Services communicate asynchronously via AMQP.

Subtask 3.2: Review API Interactions
Trace Nova API call:
nova list --debug
Examine HTTP headers:
curl -i -X GET http://<controller>:8774/v2.1/servers \
  -H "X-Auth-Token: $(openstack token issue -c id -f value)"
Subtask 3.3: Service Health Checks
systemctl list-units --type=service | grep -E 'nova|neutron|keystone'
Conclusion
Key Takeaways
OpenStack's control plane consists of multiple interconnected services
Keystone serves as the central authentication and service catalog
Services communicate via REST APIs and message queues
Proper configuration of service endpoints is critical for operation
Verification
Create a test resource to validate all services:
openstack network create test_net
openstack server create --image cirros --flavor m1.tiny --network test_net test_instance
Verify resource creation:
openstack server list
openstack network list
Cleanup
openstack server delete test_instance
openstack network delete test_net
